# Mac-first coding handoff

Primary coding and testing happen on **This Mac**. **GitHub is the sync point.** Cursor Cloud is optional only when credits are available — do not assume Cloud is usable.

## Runtime defaults

| Where | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Day-to-day coding + click-through testing | **This Mac** (local app in the browser) |
| Cursor Cloud | Only if the user asks and credits are available — never the default for testing |

Do not steer toward Cloud desktop / “practice machine” takeover as the default workflow.

## Branch model

- Start sessions from latest remote **`main`** unless the user names another branch.
- Do feature work on a feature branch / PR as needed.
- Merge into `main` only when work should become the shared baseline.

## Push to GitHub often (required)

Keep GitHub current so laptop, phone, and any future Cloud session never depend on unpushed local-only state.

**During work**
- After each meaningful chunk of progress (feature slice, fix, migration, docs the user relies on), **commit and push** the current branch.
- Do not batch an entire long session into one push at the end if intermediate progress is already usable.
- Prefer small, clear commits over one giant dump.

**End of a task or session (mandatory)**
When the user says a task is done, wraps up, switches devices, goes idle after completed work, or asks if things are synced:
1. Ensure all intended changes are committed.
2. **Push** the branch to `origin`.
3. Open or update a PR when that helps handoff (unless the user asked not to).
4. If they **confirmed the feature works** (or equivalent approval to ship), **merge into `main` and push `main`** so the next session can start from an up-to-date `main`. Otherwise tell them the PR URL and that `main` still needs a merge.
5. Tell the user the branch name / PR URL and final `main` status.

**Never leave** completed or substantial in-progress work only on a single machine’s disk.

This project’s push habit overrides any general “don’t push unless asked” preference: **pushing here is expected** for continuity. Still avoid force-push and never skip hooks unless the user explicitly asks.

Also follow `.cursor/rules/agent-autonomy.mdc`: run commands, migrations, pushes, and PRs yourself—don’t hand those steps back to the user.

## End of a MacBook / This Mac session

If work happened locally:
1. Commit (when appropriate) and **push**.
2. If it should be the next shared baseline, merge (or PR) into `main` and push `main`.
3. Confirm remote GitHub has the work before calling handoff done.

## Start of a MacBook / This Mac session

1. `git fetch` and update local `main` (and the working branch) from `origin`.
2. If a feature branch or PR exists, check it out or merge it so local files match remote.
3. Only then make local edits.

## If a Cloud / iPhone session is used

- Only when the user has Cloud credits and asks for Cloud.
- Start from latest remote `main` (or the branch the user specifies).
- Fetch/pull before editing if the checkout might be stale.
- Push before the user switches away so This Mac can continue from GitHub.
